From afd943f3115490139a639d056245b2a7be5adefc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: steamac <steamac@local>
Date: Tue, 13 Oct 2026 14:41:29 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] balloon(macos): release reported free pages instead of
 MADV_FREE

Free page reporting (VIRTIO_BALLOON_F_REPORTING, which the steamac guest
kernel negotiates: CONFIG_PAGE_REPORTING=y, order 9) handed each reported
range to madvise(MADV_FREE) on macOS. On macOS that does not uncharge the
pages: they stay in the VMM's phys_footprint until the host runs short of
memory, and guest RAM is charged once per mapping (host stage-1 and the HVF
stage-2 mapping both count), so a guest that once touched its RAM keeps the
VMM at that footprint for good (8 GiB guest: 9.3-10.5 GiB right after boot,
no lower after the guest frees memory). MADV_DONTNEED behaves the same.

The balloon now sends whole host pages of a reported range to the VMM worker
(new WorkerMessage::DiscardMemory), which unmaps them from the guest, replaces
them with a fresh anonymous mapping and maps that back
(HvfVm::discard_memory): the pages are freed at once and uncharged, and the
next guest access gets zero pages that are charged normally. The stage-2
mapping has to be rebuilt because hv_vm_map binds pages, not the host address
range. Partial host pages at the edges, ranges spanning two memory regions and
any failure keep the old MADV_FREE. Cost: only when the guest reports
(~35 us per 2 MiB block); nothing changes for guests without the feature.

Upstream state (libkrun v1.19.6 227b2de, main at the time): MADV_FREE on
macOS, MADV_DONTNEED on Linux, where it does uncharge.

Test: hvf discard_memory_releases_pages_and_keeps_the_guest_mapping (needs
the hypervisor entitlement): a guest fills 16 MiB, discard must uncharge
>= 16 MiB, read back zeros, and later guest stores must reach the new host
pages. With MADV_FREE it fails (0 bytes released), without the stage-2
remap it fails (guest stores invisible to the host).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
